# Writing teaching content (history, genres, scales)

The Musical History module (`/history`) tells *where a sound came from* as
long-form, **sourced** prose — the counterpart to Genres/Scales, which show
*how* it works. See `docs/plans/musical-history-module.md`. The blues article
(`src/content/history/BluesHistory.tsx`) is the reference implementation.

This is also our **house style for any long-form article/blog** we add later.
The shared pieces are reusable: citations (`src/components/history/`), the song
menu (`SongLink`), the inline player (`src/components/media/YouTubeEmbed.tsx`),
the theory-term glossary (`<Term>` + `src/lib/concepts/registry.ts`), loanwords
(`<Word>` / `<Term>` + `src/lib/words/registry.ts`), and the artists/songs
catalog (`<ArtistLink>` + `src/lib/catalog/`).

## Type size — 16px floor

On a long-form entry, **nothing the reader reads is smaller than 16px**.
That is Tailwind `text-base` (`1rem`). Main reading text (body paragraphs,
lead answer, lists, quotes) is **18px** (`text-lg`) via `prose.tsx`.
Captions, labels, and notes sit at the 16px floor. Section headings are
`text-xl`, and page titles are larger still.

Entries: history, cousins, genres, scales and their lessons, concepts,
songs, artists, languages, and the about page. The floor covers the whole
reading column and anything that opens from it:

- body, captions, lyrics, credits, footnotes, source lists
- section labels, related-page links, “coming soon” notes
- genre pills, loanword banner, term / artist / song popovers
- the embedded map’s heading and place names

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — 14px body, 12px caption, 10px pill
<p className="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
<p className="text-xs text-muted-foreground">
<span className="text-[10px]">Blues</span>

// ✅ GOOD — shared primitives; captions at 16px in the muted gray
<P>…</P>
<p className="mt-2 text-base text-muted-foreground">Source: …</p>
```

Do not use `text-sm`, `text-xs`, or a custom size under `1rem` for that
copy. Shared chrome that renders on these pages (`RelatedPages`, `HubLink`,
`GenrePills`, `SongLyrics`, citations, `WordBanner`) already uses
`text-base` — don’t pass a smaller size back in.

**Outside this floor:** hub index cards and their search/filters, the synth,
piano roll, MIDI Lab, and harmonica. Piano key names and the lesson octave
stepper are instrument controls, shared with those tools — leave their size
alone.

## Reading column — use `src/components/content/prose.tsx`

The readability rules are a class list, not a habit. Article bodies, scale
lessons, and entry-page prose import from `prose.tsx`. Don't hand-roll a
local `H2` / `P` or copy the class strings.

| Piece | Use | What it enforces |
|---|---|---|
| Section heading | `<H2 {...SECTIONS["id"]} />` | 20px (`text-xl`), `text-balance`, more space above (`mt-12`) than below |
| Paragraph | `<P>` / `PROSE_BODY` + `PROSE_GAP` | 18px (`text-lg`) at line-height 1.85 (`leading-[1.85]`, ~33px), 24px between paragraphs (`mt-6`), `text-foreground/85`, `max-w-[65ch]`, `text-pretty` |
| Lead answer under `<h1>` | `PROSE_LEAD` | same measure, full foreground |
| Parallel items | `<ProseList>` (`ordered` for steps) | same type as body, muted markers |
| Link that navigates | `PROSE_LINK` | always underlined, thin; darkens on hover |
| Contents | `<OnThisPage sections={SECTIONS} />` | shown at 4+ sections |

Entry `<h1>`s also carry `text-balance`.

**Line spacing is the main readability lever.** Reading prose and quotes
use `leading-[1.85]`, not `leading-relaxed` (1.625), which is for UI text,
captions, and lyrics lines. To retune, change the one value in `prose.tsx`
(and `Blockquote` in `citations.tsx`), not per page. Keep the paragraph gap
a little under one body line, so paragraphs stay separate without breaking
the flow.

### Contrast — three levels, fixed meanings

- **Headings:** `text-foreground`, semibold.
- **Body you read:** `text-foreground/85`.
- **Notes about the text:** `text-muted-foreground`. That means captions,
  credits, source lists, labels, fair-use footers, and metadata lines.

Never set a paragraph of reading in the muted gray. In light mode it is
about 4.6:1, which is legal but tiring across a whole article.

### Line length

Hold prose to about 65 characters (`max-w-[65ch]`, already in
`PROSE_BODY`). Players, maps, lists of cards, and tables may use the full
column width.

### Paragraph and heading rhythm (writing)

- One idea per paragraph, **2–4 sentences, never more than ~90 words**. If
  a paragraph needs a second “also,” split it.
- A new `H2` every **3–5 paragraphs**. A heading names what the section
  says (“The first record we can point at”), not a category (“Background”).
- Registry prose (`about`, `bio`, `definition`) follows the same rule. Split
  paragraphs with a blank line (`\n\n`), which the pages already render.

### Lists and quotes (writing)

- **Three or more parallel items** (traits, instruments, places, steps) go in
  `<ProseList>`, not a long comma sentence. Two items stay in the sentence.
- A list item is a phrase or one sentence. If it needs two, it is a paragraph.
- **Quotes: about three lines or fewer** in `<Blockquote>`. For longer
  passages, quote the key line and paraphrase the rest, with a footnote.

### Emphasis (writing)

- *Italics* are for titles of works and for foreign words (the `<em>` inside
  `<Word>` / `<Term>`). Don't use italics for stress.
- **Bold** only for the term a lesson is defining, and at most once per
  section (“into **harmonic minor**”). Never bold a whole sentence.
- ALL CAPS with wide letter-spacing only on short labels (“Refrain,”
  “Public domain,” a language tag). Never on a sentence or a heading.
- No exclamation marks, and no emphasis added to other people's quotes.

### Links — two looks, two meanings

- **Solid thin underline (`PROSE_LINK`)** means the link goes somewhere:
  another page, or out to a source.
- **Dotted underline** means it opens a popover in place: `<Term>`,
  `<ArtistLink>`. The song pill (`<SongLink>`) keeps its own look.
- Link the first meaningful mention. There is no cap on links per paragraph.
  Link text says where it goes (“the blues scale”), never “here” or “this page.”
- External sources go through `<Cite>` or the source list, so they can be
  checked, rather than as bare inline URLs.

### Contents

A history or cousin article with **four or more sections** defines them once
and gets a contents line next to “Songs in this article”:

```tsx
const SECTIONS = defineSections({
  "predecessors": "Predecessors: West Africa, and what else it borrowed",
  "roots": "Work songs, field hollers, spirituals",
  // …in page order
});

<div className="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-start gap-x-8 gap-y-2">
  <OnThisPage sections={SECTIONS} />
  <SongJumpNav ids={SONG_IDS} />
</div>
<H2 {...SECTIONS["predecessors"]} />
```

Scale lessons keep their numbered `H2`s (the numbers are the contents).

## The moving parts (do all of these to ship an article)

1. **Registry entry** — `src/lib/history/registry.ts`: `slug`, `question`
   (the `<h1>` + title), `summary` (hub card), `answer` (quotable lead —
   mirrored into the meta description + FAQ JSON-LD, so it must be **true
   standalone**), `genres`/`scales` (data cross-links), `sources`, `keywords`.
2. **Body** — `src/content/history/<Name>.tsx`, a **server component** (no
   `"use client"`) so quotes and footnotes ship in the static HTML.
3. **Register it** — add the slug → component to `src/content/history/index.ts`.
4. **Flip `status` to `"live"`** only once the body + real sources exist.
   `"live"` enters the sitemap and search index; a live page with no body is
   just a lead sentence.

## Cousins — one melody, many lives

Authoring standard: **`.cursor/rules/cousins.mdc`**. Catalog songs:
**`.cursor/rules/catalog-songs.mdc`**.

`/cousins` uses the same article chrome as history. `genres` is required
and renders as `<GenrePills>` on the hub and spoke. Every named song is a
catalog member **and** lists `cousins: ["<slug>"]`; the song page shows
that link at the **top**. Don’t put lyric blocks on the cousin page.
Titles and link labels are locked — **One melody, many lives** /
**History & versions of {name}**. Use `cousinQuestion` /
`COUSIN_SONG_HEADING` from the cousins registry; never “Where else does
this tune live?”. See `.cursor/rules/cousins.mdc`.

## Bidirectional links — three trees, always both ways

Genres, scales, and history are siblings. A reader on any spoke must be
able to walk to the other two. **Cross-links are data**, then the page
renders them — never a one-off `<Link>` that the reverse page doesn't
know about.

| From | Declare on | Reverse lookup |
|---|---|---|
| Genre → scales | `Genre.scales` | `ScaleLesson.usedIn` |
| Scale → genres | `ScaleLesson.usedIn` | `Genre.scales` |
| History → genres / scales | `HistoryArticle.genres` / `.scales` | `getArticleByGenre` / `getArticlesByScale` |
| Cousin → songs | `CousinArticle.members` | `CatalogSong.cousins` + `getCousinsBySong` |
| Song → cousins | `CatalogSong.cousins` | `CousinArticle.members` |

**When you add one side, add the other in the same change.** A scale that
shows up in blues (`usedIn: ["blues"]`) must also appear on the blues
genre (`scales: […, "that-slug"]`). A history article that discusses the
blues scale must list `"blues-scale"` in `scales` so `/scales/blues-scale`
grows a "Where did the blues come from?" card.

Render those links with **`<RelatedPages>`**
(`src/components/content/RelatedPages.tsx`) — the shared card list used
by genre, scale, history, and concept spokes. Don't hand-roll a second
card style. History pages show this list **even after the article body
exists** ("Take it apart"); don't hide the reverse links behind `!Article`.

Also link inline in article prose (first meaningful mention) to
`/genres/<slug>` and `/scales/<slug>`. Inline links teach; the related
footer is the guaranteed way back. `"soon"` spokes still get links —
placeholders are doors, not dead ends.

Concepts and catalog entries follow the same graph: if a concept or song
belongs in the blues story, list `genres` / `scales` / `history` on that
entry so `/concepts/<slug>`, `/songs/<slug>`, and `/artists/<slug>` grow
the same cards.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — history knows the scale, the scale page can't find the article
// history: { scales: ["blues-scale"] }
// scale:   { usedIn: [] }          // genre missing
// genre:   { scales: [] }          // scale missing

// ✅ GOOD — both directions, same slugs
// history: { genres: ["blues"], scales: ["blues-scale", "minor-pentatonic"] }
// scale:   { usedIn: ["blues", "rock"] }
// genre:   { scales: ["blues-scale", "minor-pentatonic"] }
```

## Sourcing — earn every claim

- **Public-domain / openly-licensed first** (Library of Congress, National
  Jukebox, archive.org, PD texts). Flag them `publicDomain: true`.
- **Never fabricate** a URL, author, title, date, or quote. If you can't
  verify it, **web-search to confirm** before citing — don't cite from memory.
- **Quote briefly** for commentary, always with attribution and a link out.
  Never reproduce a whole article. Non-PD works are fair use — note it (e.g.
  `access: "Quoted briefly under fair use"`).
- Keep the closing fair-use/attribution note at the foot of the article.

## Citations — use the shared primitives

Import from `src/components/history/citations.tsx`; don't hand-roll footnotes.

- **`<Cite id sources />`** — inline `[n]`. Numbering derives from the source's
  position in the registry `sources` array, so inline refs and the bibliography
  can't drift. The `[n]` links straight to the source URL (new tab) when it has
  one, else jumps to the bibliography anchor.
- **`<Blockquote attribution cite sources>`** — short attributed quotes. The
  **whole attribution line + `[n]`** is one clickable link (a big hit target),
  via `<Cite label={attribution} …>`. Don't render a bare attribution string.
- **`<SourceList sources />`** — the numbered bibliography; render it once at
  the end. Order the `sources` array in first-appearance order.
- Bind them to the article once: `const cite = (id) => <Cite id={id} sources={SOURCES} />`.

## Songs get a menu to hear / open them

Any **song named** in an article uses **`<SongLink id="<slug>" />`**
(`src/components/history/SongLink.tsx`). It takes only the catalog slug — title,
attribution, YouTube id, and Piano Roll link all come from the catalog (see the
catalog section below). The title sits inline with a **YouTube icon** (YouTube
red — `text-red-600` — so it reads as “play a video”). Clicking it opens a
**full-width in-flow panel** (same width as the prose column — it pushes the
body down; never an overlay) with:

- **Open in Piano Roll** — `/piano-roll/<pianoRollId>`, opens a **new tab** and
  shows a trailing **arrow**; **greyed out, no arrow** when the catalog song has
  no `pianoRollId` (i.e. it isn't in the MIDI library `src/lib/songs`).
- **Play on YouTube** — a chip (YouTube mark + label) that expands the
  reusable `YouTubeEmbed` (`src/components/media/YouTubeEmbed.tsx`), so
  the user stays on the page. Song pages and cousins articles use
  `CollapsibleVideo` for that chip. Do not mount an open iframe as the
  recording itself. See “Players” in `.cursor/rules/catalog-songs.mdc`.
- **About “<song>” / More about <artist>** — cross-links to `/songs/<slug>` and
  each `/artists/<slug>`, so the mention is a doorway into the catalog.

Multiple songs can be open at once, and an open panel **stays open** (and its
player keeps playing) until the user deliberately closes it — toggling the
title again or pressing Escape — or navigates away. Don't reintroduce an
outside-click / blur collapse: a stray click anywhere used to unmount the
iframe and kill playback. But only **one plays at a time**: a newly
opened player autoplays only if nothing else is sounding, and hitting play on a
second song pauses the first (the `YouTubeEmbed`s cooperate via
`youtubeConductor.ts` — don't reintroduce a bare autoplay iframe). List the
article's song slugs in a `SONG_IDS` array and pass them to
**`<SongJumpNav ids={SONG_IDS} />`**. Don't wrap a title **inside a direct
quotation**. Offer a clip as a `CollapsibleVideo` chip. `YouTubeEmbed` is
the player inside that chip, not a bare iframe dropped onto the page.

## People are not bylines

A song, artist, genre, or history entry that names a person has to know
them. Dates and a credit line are not enough. Hunt the life, then keep
the few facts that make the work make sense: a job, a family, a journey,
a joke in a title, a contradiction. Reisen is the example — four years in
a Russian army band, a house full of writers, a secular poet whose Sukkot
poem Hasidim still sing, a first story titled “Damn the nose, so long as
there is a dowry of a gold watch and 300 rubles.”

- The **artist** `bio` holds the life. A song or genre page gets a short
  passage when that person is who the entry is about — not a pasted
  biography, and not “he was a famous writer.”
- Every fact is sourced (an encyclopedia, a book, their own memoir). If
  the army years or the anecdote cannot be verified, leave them out. Do
  not invent color to make a page lively.
- Prefer the concrete: a title, a year, a place, what they actually did.
  One odd true detail is worth more than a paragraph of praise.
- A genre gets this when a person is how that shelf is explained. A genre
  with no such person does not get a made-up hero.

## Artists & songs get catalog entries (cross-referencing)

Songs and the people who made them are **shared entities**, not article-local
data. They live in the editorial catalog **`src/lib/catalog/`** so every mention
across the app — this article, a genre page, another article — resolves to the
same entry and cross-links back. (This is separate from `src/lib/songs`, the
MIDI arrangements the Piano Roll plays; a catalog song points into it via
`pianoRollId`.)

- **`songs.ts`** — a `CatalogSong` per recording/composition: `slug`, `title`,
  `artists` (artist slugs in credit order), `artistLabel?`, `year`, `youtubeId`
  (**verified**, never from memory), `pianoRollId?` (only when it's a real id in
  the MIDI library), `micro`, `lyrics?` (see below), `genres`/`history` slugs,
  and `cousins` when a `/cousins` article names the song (see
  `.cursor/rules/catalog-songs.mdc`). The song page shows those cousin
  links at the top.
- **`artists.ts`** — an `Artist` per person: `slug`, `name`, `era`, `micro`
  (one-line bio, true standalone), longer `bio`, `genres`/`history` slugs,
  **`places`** (every city the bio names — see “Map” in
  `.cursor/rules/catalog-songs.mdc`), and
  external `links` (encyclopedias/archives — verify URLs, don't fabricate).
  Those links are the page’s **Sources** list, rendered last by
  `<EntrySources>`, not a mid-page “Elsewhere” block. When a Wikipedia
  article exists for the song or the person, it is one of those links.
  See `.cursor/rules/catalog-songs.mdc` (“Sources — the bottom of the page”).
  Don't list an artist's songs; `songsByArtist()` derives them.
- **Name a person in prose → our artist page, or Wikipedia.** If they
  are in `artists.ts` (or this change is adding them), wrap every mention
  in `<ArtistLink id="<slug>">Name</ArtistLink>` (`src/components/catalog/`),
  the artist counterpart to `<Term>`: dotted underline, click for a micro-bio
  + link to `/artists/<slug>`. Every mention, not only the first. If they
  have no entry and we are not adding one, the name links to their Wikipedia
  article when one exists. Full rule:
  `.cursor/rules/person-names.mdc`. Concepts and loanwords stay
  first-mention only.
  Registry strings (map place blurbs, genre `about`, scale `history`, song
  `about`, artist `bio`) go through **`makeTermLinker()`**, which wraps every
  catalog `name` and `alsoNamed` form automatically, and turns
  `[Sarah](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarah_Reisen)` into the Wikipedia
  link. Hand-authored TSX still needs `<ArtistLink>` or a `PROSE_LINK`
  anchor on each mention. If the prose uses a surname alone (“Reisen”),
  put it on `alsoNamed` — only when that form won’t match someone else.
- Adding a song or artist is just a registry append — the `/songs` and
  `/artists` hubs, spoke pages, and sitemap pick it up automatically. Set
  `status: "live"` only once the entry is real.
- Keep the catalog non-duplicative: reuse an existing `slug` rather than
  inventing a second entry for the same song/person.

### Lyrics belong on the song page

When you add or expand a catalog song, **source the lyrics and show them**
on `/songs/<slug>`. Hearing the recording without the words is half a
lesson. Store them on `CatalogSong.lyrics` (`SongLyrics[]`) and render
with **`<SongLyrics title={song.title} versions={song.lyrics ?? []} recordings={songPageRecordings(song)} />`** — don't hand-roll a second
layout, and don't play the recordings again above that section.
Each version is **one card**: the recording, then the words it sings.
A recording names its version with `lyrics: "<id>"`. More than one
card starts **collapsed**; the words stay in the HTML. The heading
names lyrics, translation, and recordings as the page actually has
them. Before the history, an **On this page** list starts with **Song
history** (`#history`), then the jump to `#lyrics` with that same
wording and a speaker icon at the end of the link. A rule and space
separate that list from the history.
See `.cursor/rules/catalog-songs.mdc` (“Lyrics and recordings —
one section, one card per version”).
**Copyright is not a reason to skip** — see
`.cursor/rules/catalog-songs.mdc` (“Lyrics — always hunt”). Over the
Rainbow shipped empty because an agent treated 1938 copyright as a stop;
that is forbidden.

- **Hunt before you give up.** Scholarly song collections (Mlotek / Workers
  Circle for Yiddish), **Yiddish Song of the Week**
  ([yiddishsong.wordpress.com](https://yiddishsong.wordpress.com/))
  for any Yiddish song — field recordings, history notes, variants, and
  often words plus a translation; search the blog and read the comments
  (see `.cursor/rules/catalog-songs.mdc`) — Library of Congress /
  National Jukebox, Wikisource / IMSLP, PD songbooks, published sheet
  music, a cited encyclopedia table that actually prints the words.
  Web-search; don't quote from memory.
  Still-protected lyrics: reprint from a publication we can point at,
  attribute, link out; the component already prints the teaching/fair-use
  note. Omit only for an instrumental, an author’s explicit no-reprint
  request, or a hunt that found no citable text — and say so in `about`.
- **Non-English songs: original + line-by-line English.** Each line keeps the
  home-language `text` (native script via `<NativeScript>`), optional `latin`
  romanization when the original isn't Latin, and `en` (a literal gloss, not
  a rewrite). One sung line → one English line.
- **Several versions → several entries.** A Yiddish original and a later
  English folk version are two `SongLyrics` objects on the same song (see
  `dona-dona`). English-only versions omit `en`. When a song is sung in more
  than one language, add a labeled `recordings` entry for each so the page
  can play a Yiddish example *and* an English one — don't leave only the
  crossover hit. `youtubeId` stays the `<SongLink>` default.
- **Always credit the source** (`source.label` + verified `url`). Public-
  domain first (`publicDomain` on the source when true). Still-protected
  lyrics: reproduce only from a publication we can point at, attribute, link
  out, and keep the teaching/fair-use note the component already prints.
  Don't paste a lyric site with no provenance.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — remembered, unsourced, or English only for a Yiddish song
lyrics: [{ label: "Yiddish", lang: "yi", language: "Yiddish", lines: [
  { text: "On a wagon bound for market" },
]}]

// ✅ GOOD — sourced original + line-by-line English, plus the later version
lyrics: [
  { id: "yiddish", label: "Yiddish original", lang: "yi", language: "Yiddish",
    source: { label: "Mlotek Yiddish Song Collection", url: "https://…" },
    lines: [{ text: "אױפֿן פֿורל ליגט דאָס קעלבל", latin: "Oyfn furl ligt dos kelbl",
              en: "Upon the wagon lies the calf" }] },
  { id: "english", label: "English (Kevess & Schwartz)", lang: "en",
    language: "English", source: { label: "…", url: "https://…" },
    lines: [{ text: "On a wagon bound for market" }] },
]
```

## Theory terms get an inline definition

When prose names a **music-theory concept** (pentatonic, blue notes,
syncopation, 12-bar blues, shuffle…), wrap it in **`<Term id="…">`**
(`src/components/concepts/Term.tsx`). The word gets a quiet dotted underline;
clicking pops a one-line definition + a **"Full explanation →"** link. It's
phrasing-level and a transient overlay (text only) — it never pushes the
layout, unlike `SongLink`.

- **Data lives in `src/lib/concepts/registry.ts`**, one `Concept` per term:
  `micro` (the popover blurb — true standalone), optional longer `definition`
  (glossary page body), `href`, and `scales`/`genres`/`history` cross-links.
- **Two homes for "learn more."** If a term already has a richer page, set
  `href` to it (e.g. `pentatonic` → `/scales/minor-pentatonic`) and it gets
  **no** glossary stub. Otherwise it's glossary-owned: `href` is
  `conceptHome(slug)` (`/concepts/<slug>`) and the `/concepts/[slug]` spoke
  renders `definition` + "See it in action" cross-links. `GLOSSARY_CONCEPTS`
  drives `generateStaticParams`; `LIVE_CONCEPTS` drives the sitemap.
- **Link the first meaningful mention**, not every occurrence — over-linking
  reads as noise. Pass children matching the prose
  (`<Term id="syncopation">off-beat accent</Term>`); the `id` does the lookup.
- **Chasidic is the exception, and the spelling.** Write **Chasidic**
  (a follower is a **Chasid**, the people **Chasidim**). **Hasidic** is an
  alternate and a search phrase, not the word the sentence uses. Rule:
  `.cursor/rules/chasidic-spelling.mdc`. Every mention — in prose and in
  lyric notes — links to the glossary entry for the movement
  (`<Term id="chasidic">`), not to Wikipedia and not only the first time.
  The glossary page is the brief history and meaning; its sources list
  links to Wikipedia. Do not send the word straight out.
- **Terms *may* go inside a direct quotation** — unlike `<SongLink>`. When a
  quotation itself names a concept (Kubik's *"characteristic pentatonic
  system"*), that's a prime teaching moment: link it. The popover is a
  transient text overlay showing *our* glossary blurb + "Full explanation →",
  so it never alters the quoted words or puts them in the author's mouth. A
  vivid quote counts as a meaningful mention worth a widget even if the concept
  also appears in nearby body prose — don't leave the quote bare just to avoid a
  second underline. (This is the one place we deliberately relax "first mention
  only.") `<SongLink>` stays out of quotations — it opens a disruptive panel.
- **Adding a concept:** append to `CONCEPTS` with a verified, plain-language
  `micro` (never fabricate theory), and list its cross-links. New glossary-
  owned terms are indexed automatically — no other wiring needed. Set the
  optional `question` when `What is <term>?` reads badly (plurals/multi-word,
  e.g. `steps` → "What are half steps and whole steps?"); `conceptQuestion()`
  falls back to the default and feeds the `<h1>`, `<title>`, and FAQ JSON-LD.
- **A concept page can carry an interactive demo.** For a concept you can
  *play*, add a client widget to `src/content/concepts/` and map it in
  `src/content/concepts/index.ts` (`getConceptContent(slug)`); the spoke renders
  it in a "Play it" section between the definition and cross-links — same split
  as scale lessons, so the registry stays free of client components. `steps`
  is the reference: a one-octave **v2** `KeyboardV2` (never the v1 synth — see
  the no-v1 rule) with half/whole-step buttons. Playing is the audio consent.
- Reuse `<Term>` anywhere theory terms appear (genres, scales, lessons), not
  just history.

## Loanwords get a home spelling and a voice

When prose uses a word from another language — Yiddish *krechtz*, Romanian
*doina*, Hebrew *Ahava Rabbah* — **always** register it and show the native
spelling **in the same change**. Don't leave a transliteration hanging; the
reader should see how it's written at home. Write-time checklist + how to
run a periodic scan: **`.cursor/rules/loanword-speakers.mdc`**.

- **Data lives in `src/lib/words/registry.ts`**, one `SpokenWord` per
  loanword: `latin` (how we write it in English), `alsoSpelled` (1–2
  common English variants the reader will meet — *krechtz* / *krekhts*),
  optional `aliases` (plurals and linker-only phrases), `native`
  (`spelling`, `language`, `lang` BCP-47), and optional `audio` (see
  below). Reuse an existing `id` rather than inventing a second entry for
  the same word. If English spelling varies, fill `alsoSpelled` — the
  banner and Term popover show them.

### Pronunciation is a real human recording, or nothing

- **Never synthesize speech.** The speaker button plays a verified human
  recording only. A word with no recording shows its spelling with **no
  speaker** — that's correct, not a gap to paper over with TTS.
- **Set `audio` only after verifying** it's a real human saying the *matching*
  word/sense and is openly licensed; then self-host in
  `public/audio/words/<id>.mp3` and credit it in
  `public/audio/words/ATTRIBUTION.md`. If you can't verify one, leave `audio`
  off — never fabricate a URL or ship a synthetic voice.
- **Where to find clips per language + how to verify** lives in its own rule:
  `.cursor/rules/pronunciation-audio.mdc`. Update that catalog whenever you
  add a language or find/rule-out a source.
- **Never fabricate a spelling.** Use an attested form, or the standard
  orthography of an attested Latin word (YIVO for Yiddish, Romanian
  diacritics, unpointed Hebrew for prayer names). Cite the source in a
  short comment on the entry. If you can't verify it, skip the native
  script — don't guess letters.
- **Theory terms that are loanwords** (`krechtz`, `doina`, `freygish`):
  add the word with the **same id as the concept slug**. `<Term>` looks it
  up and appends `(קרעכץ)` + the speaker *if a recording exists*. No extra
  JSX.
- **Loanwords that aren't concepts** (`klezmer`, `freylekhs`, `hora`):
  wrap the first meaningful mention in **`<Word id="klezmer">klezmer</Word>`**
  (`src/components/words/Word.tsx`). Same first-mention rule as `<Term>`.
  Keep `<Word>` **out of direct quotations** (it's a control, like
  `<SongLink>`).
- **Spoke pages** (concept / genre / scale / history) pick up a banner
  automatically when `getWord(slug)` hits — don't hand-roll a second
  spelling line in the header.
- **Hebrew / Yiddish next to English:** never put `dir="rtl"` on a box
  that also holds Latin (language name, parentheses, "also spelled").
  That flips `ms-*` and glues *Yiddish* to *קרעכץ*. Render the letters
  with `<NativeScript>` (`src/components/words/NativeScript.tsx`); space
  with an LTR flex `gap` or `ms-*` on an LTR wrapper.
- **Registry strings** (`genre.about`, scale `history`, history `answer`,
  place `music.blurb`) go through **`makeTermLinker()`**, which also wraps
  first mentions of registered words and catalog artist names. Adding the
  word to `WORDS` (with specific aliases) or the artist to `ARTISTS` is
  enough for those pages. Hand-authored TSX still needs `<Word>` /
  `<Term>` / `<ArtistLink>`.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — transliteration only
the little sobbing <em>krechtz</em>

// ✅ GOOD — concept loanword (Term shows native + listen)
the little sobbing <Term id="krechtz">krechtz</Term>

// ✅ GOOD — not a concept
<Word id="freylekhs">freylekhs</Word>
```

## Registry prose gets terms and loanwords via the auto-linker

Hand-written `<Term>` only works in TSX. The genre `about`/`answer`, scale
`history`/`answer`, and map place `music.blurb` are **plain strings** in the
registries, so they can't hold JSX. To light those up, render them through
**`makeTermLinker()`** (`src/components/concepts/autoTerm.tsx`) instead of
printing the raw string:

```tsx
const linkTerms = makeTermLinker();   // one per page
<p>{linkTerms(genre.answer)}</p>
<p>{linkTerms(genre.about)}</p>
```

- It wraps the **first occurrence** of each registry concept (matched on `term`
  + `aliases`, longest phrase wins) in a `<Term>`, and each other loanword in
  a `<Word>`. A catalog artist is different: **every** mention of `name` or
  `alsoNamed` becomes an `<ArtistLink>`. **One linker per page** — share it
  across the lead and body so a concept or word links once, not once per
  paragraph. Artists are not part of that once-per-page budget.
- It's a plain function returning nodes (no hooks), so pages **stay server
  components** and the prose text still ships in static HTML. `<Term>` /
  `<Word>` / `<ArtistLink>` are the only client leaves.
- It's **opt-in**: a page must call it. Don't auto-link deep in shared UI where
  an author can't see what lit up. Registry descriptions are our own prose (not
  quotations), so there's no direct-quote exception to worry about — but if you
  add a field that can contain a quotation, don't run it through the linker.
- Widening coverage is cheap: **add the concept to `CONCEPTS`**, the
  loanword to `WORDS` (with sensible `aliases`), and/or the person to
  `ARTISTS`, and every registry description that mentions them lights up.
  Keep aliases specific enough not to false-match common words. Artist
  names match the catalog `name` only — write the full name in the blurb
  ("Abe Schwartz", not "Schwartz").

## Reader navigation & motion

- Render **`<SongJumpNav songs={SONGS} />`** at the top of the body (just under
  the lead). It's a **quiet, collapsible contents line** (muted text +
  chevron — *not* a boxed card), so it reads as part of the essay.
- Jumping to a song scrolls it to the **vertical center** of the viewport
  (`block: "center"`), not under the header, and updates the URL hash.
- On arrival the song title plays a short **"find me" highlight** (~2.8s soft
  glow + underline) so the eye can locate it in the prose. Honor
  `prefers-reduced-motion` (via `useReducedMotion`) — show a static marked
  state instead of the pulse. Timing follows the calm-motion house rules.

## Predecessors — every genre has them; name them

Almost every genre evolved out of earlier music. **Every history article
must include a predecessors section** (near the top, before the genre
“takes its familiar shape”) that:

1. **Names the earlier traditions** — scales, forms, practices, regions —
   that fed the sound. Don’t collapse them into a slogan (“from Mali to
   Mississippi”). Name traits (call-and-response, pentatonic pitch,
   bent notes, a dance rhythm) and say they were *carried and remade*,
   not that the later genre already existed overseas.
2. **Links across the app** whenever a predecessor (or descendant) has a
   page — even a `"soon"` placeholder. Typical targets:
   - `/scales/<slug>` for a predecessor or related scale
   - `/genres/<slug>` for a parent, foil, or child genre
   - `/history/<slug>` for that genre’s history article, if one exists
   - `/scales` or `/genres` hubs when the specific spoke isn’t written yet
3. **Covers other inspirations too** — not only the main ancestral line
   (e.g. Anglo-European ballads and guitar harmony next to West African
   pitch practice). Same rule: cite, don’t invent a single-origin myth.

If we don’t yet have a page for a predecessor, still name it and cite
an external source. When that page lands, come back and add the link.

## Voice & honesty (Instrumaps: calm, precise, honest)

- **Don't launder legend as fact.** Where the popular story is contested, say
  so and cite the scholarship — e.g. the blues was not simply "born in the
  Delta from hardship."
- **Distinguish ancestry from category.** A thing can be *secular* (what the
  music is about) yet descend from *sacred* sources (spirituals). Name the
  distinction rather than letting the words look contradictory.
- Prose is plain TSX + Tailwind (no `prose` plugin). Use the primitives in
  `src/components/content/prose.tsx`. See “Type size” and “Reading column”
  above. Link inline to the matching `/genres/<slug>` and `/scales/<slug>`
  pages with `PROSE_LINK`.

## Keep the short blurbs in sync

The article is the source of truth. When it establishes a fact, reconcile the
**shorter versions** so the app never contradicts itself: the genre `answer` /
`about` (`src/lib/genres/registry.ts`), the scale `history`
(`src/lib/scales/registry.ts`), and this article's own `summary` / `answer`.
After a new claim, also sweep the concept `micro`/`definition` and catalog
`micro`/`bio` that retell it.

## Before you finish

- A new or expanded catalog song has sourced `lyrics` (original + line-by-line
  English when it isn't English; each sung version is its own entry). Copyright
  is not a skip. Omit only for instrumental / author forbade reprint / hunt
  failed, and say so in `about`.
- A live song or artist whose prose names places has `places` on that
  entry, and any new city is in `src/lib/places/registry.ts` **without** a
  broad genre tag. `yiddish-folk` does not draw a map by itself. See
  “Map” in `.cursor/rules/catalog-songs.mdc`.
- Jewish repertoire (traditional Jewish, Chasidic, Yiddish, klezmer,
  Yiddish theater) pins `"israel"` and names it once in the prose — the
  song is sung there now. Songs, artists, genres, cousins alike.
- A song, artist, or genre spoke ends with `<EntrySources>` — the last
  block, numbered, same list as `<SourceList>`. `links` holds the
  printings and essays; a live page does not ship with “not listed yet.”
  History and cousins articles keep `<SourceList>` at the end of the body.
- Both sides of every new cross-link exist (genre `scales` ↔ scale `usedIn`;
  history `genres`/`scales` ↔ `getArticleByGenre` / `getArticlesByScale`;
  cousin `members` ↔ song `cousins`). A live cousin article has `genres`
  and shows pills. A song named in a cousin article links that article at
  the top of `/songs/<slug>`.
- The spoke page actually *renders* those links via `<RelatedPages>` (or the
  genre header history line). A registry field with no UI is an unfinished
  link.
- Every new loanword is in `WORDS` with a verified native spelling, and its
  first mention in hand-authored TSX is wrapped (`<Term>` if it's also a
  concept, otherwise `<Word>`). `audio` only if a verified human clip exists
  (see `.cursor/rules/pronunciation-audio.mdc`); otherwise no speaker.
- If you added a **language**, found/ruled-out a **source**, or shipped a
  **clip**, update `.cursor/rules/pronunciation-audio.mdc` in this change.
- `npx tsc --noEmit`, and check lints on touched files.
- The body must stay server-rendered (crawlable). Don't add `"use client"`
  just to embed a widget — lift the widget into its own client component.
